ative to triptans, making it suitable for patients with cardiovascular risk factors. It acts directly on the central nervous system to relieve migraine symptoms but is associated with side effects such as dizziness, sedation, and fatigue. Its efficacy was established through Phase II and Phase III clinical trials, supporting its role in both stepped and stratified treatment approaches. [Data: Entities (155, 156, 166, 159); Relationships (200, 201, 202, 198, 155)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Lasmiditan is a novel, FDA-approved treatment for acute migraine with a unique mechanism and side effect profile\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Medication-overuse headache (MOH) is a significant clinical concern linked to the excessive use of acute migraine medications. The risk is particularly high with opioids (used more than 10 days/month), NSAIDs (more than 15 days/month), and caffeine-containing combination drugs (more than 10 days/month). MOH can lead to chronic daily headaches and worsen the underlying condition, creating a cycle of dependency and increased headache frequency. Preventing MOH requires strict adherence to dosing guidelines and patient education on medication use. [Data: Entities (519, 161); Relationships (648, 654, 205)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Medication-overuse headache is a major risk associated with excessive use of acute migraine medications\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Pain severity assessment tools such as the Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) and Numerical Rating Scale (NRS) are integral to evaluating the intensity of acute migraine attacks. These tools help clinicians determine the appropriate treatment strategy by quantifying patient-reported pain levels. The use of standardized scales ensures consistency in clinical decision-making and enables objective tracking of treatment response over time. Both tools are widely used in clinical practice and research settings. [Data: Entities (106, 107,